# The biome of each of the twelve territories of the first release.
#
# `spec/planet.md` requires every territory to have a biome, and says a territory's biome is
# what the terrain gives it. On a generated world that is exactly what happens: `create
# planet` reads the terrain and hands one in per territory.
#
# A designed scenario is not a generated world. These twelve were chosen in
# `releases/first-release.md` to exercise different consequences of the rules - territory 5
# has food at density 1 so that a territory which can never build anything is demonstrated
# deliberately rather than produced by accident - and the terrain knows nothing about that.
# So the designer says, which is what `set biome` is for and why it is design-phase only.
#
# Each is the biome whose character in `releases/first-release.md` -> Biomes best matches
# what that territory was built to exercise. The nodes are hand-placed and the biome table
# is a generator's, so the two are allowed to disagree; nothing reads a biome yet.
#
# None of them is ocean. Ocean cannot be claimed, and the release's loop spreads a pioneer
# across the whole planet.

# 1: the landing site; everything works
{set-biome territory:1 biome:grassland}
# 2: few dense food nodes - the first territory a pioneer reaches
{set-biome territory:2 biome:grassland}
# 3: many thin food nodes
{set-biome territory:3 biome:grassland}
# 4: the minimum a territory can be
{set-biome territory:4 biome:mountain}
# 5: food density 1, so it can never build
{set-biome territory:5 biome:mountain}
# 6: no metal
{set-biome territory:6 biome:jungle}
# 7: no energy
{set-biome territory:7 biome:jungle}
# 8: population without industry
{set-biome territory:8 biome:grassland}
# 9: rich metal, too few hands to work it
{set-biome territory:9 biome:mountain}
# 10: an energy depot
{set-biome territory:10 biome:desert}
# 11: the prize
{set-biome territory:11 biome:grassland}
# 12: rich nodes, almost no workers
{set-biome territory:12 biome:ice}
